SaltsSerial examples
====================

These examples use the public SaltsSerial ABI from `serial/include/serial.h`,
included as <serial.h> and linked through Salts::Serial. They do not include
private headers or call libserialport APIs.

Examples
--------

- `list_ports.c` - enumerates visible port names.
- `port_info.c` - reads metadata for one named port.
- `port_config.c` - configures and opens one handle.
- `send_receive.c` - performs blocking write/read.
- `await_events.c` - waits for readiness through an event set.
- `handle_errors.c` - handles result codes at the application boundary.

Build
-----

Run from the SaltsCore root after loading the configured .env and compiler
runtime environment. These commands use the Windows llvm-mingw user preset;
other platforms use their matching versioned user presets.

```sh
cmake --preset native-release-user
cmake --build --preset native-release-user
```

All six examples are part of the regular build graph. Their target names are
`saltscore_serial_example_<source-stem>`. For local work on just one example:

```sh
cmake --build --preset native-release-user --target saltscore_serial_example_list_ports
```

Run
---

Windows executables for this preset are in `build/native-release-user/bin`:

```text
build/native-release-user/bin/saltscore_serial_example_list_ports.exe
build/native-release-user/bin/saltscore_serial_example_port_info.exe COM1
build/native-release-user/bin/saltscore_serial_example_port_config.exe COM3
build/native-release-user/bin/saltscore_serial_example_send_receive.exe COM3
build/native-release-user/bin/saltscore_serial_example_await_events.exe COM3
```

Most examples require real serial hardware or a configured virtual port, so
these programs are built but are not registered as unattended CTest cases.

Validation
----------

Formal public-API, injected-backend and storage tests run through CTest:

```sh
ctest --preset native-release-user -R '^saltscore[.]serial_'
```

Unix builds additionally run the native pseudo-terminal regression under the
same filter. Physical configuration and disconnect behavior require hardware
fixtures.
